From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:45:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media (ohos): Read the media resource from the media cache

The OpenHarmony AVPlayer backend kept its own window of the media
resource, which the media element pushed bytes into: a playback buffer
that asked the element to stop and resume pushing, and to restart the
fetch at the position of a read outside the window, on a thread of its
own that waited for the element to answer a SeekData event. The media
cache now owns the bytes and the fetches, so implement
`Player::set_data_source` and let the `readAt` callback of the AVPlayer
data source read from the `MediaReader`: its data is returned as is, a
pending read returns 0, after which AVPlayer reads again 100 to 500 ms
later, the end of the resource returns -1 (`SOURCE_ERROR_EOF`), and an
error returns -2 (`SOURCE_ERROR_IO`), which ends playback with an error
that the error callback reports to the media element. A read never
blocks, so AVPlayer never waits for the script thread.

AVPlayer reads the size of the data source only when it gets it, and
treats a source without a size as a live stream. Hand it the data
source once the reader knows the length of the resource: at once if
it already does, or once the media element reports the metadata of the
resource with `set_seekable` and `set_input_size`. A length that is
still unknown then makes the data source a live stream, which AVPlayer
reads in sequence without positions, so keep the position of those
reads in the data source. The data source and its reader are dropped
with the AVPlayer wrapper, after AVPlayer is released, which waits for
the running reads.

When the reader has no data at a position yet, readAt waits up to 1 s
for it, woken by the reader's waker, by closing the reader or by
releasing AVPlayer, before it returns 0: AVPlayer gives up after about
20 empty reads within ~4.6 s. The data source is owned through a raw
pointer, so that no `&mut` to it lives while AVPlayer reads, and the
element takes origin_clean from the reader before handing it over,
since AVPlayer may render a frame of a cached tainted resource before
its metadata event reaches the element.

Remove the playback buffer and its tests, the EnoughData, NeedData and
SeekData events and the seek lock. `push_data` returns
`PlayerError::NotSupported`, except with the dummy data source below
API 21, where `set_data_source` is not supported and the element
pushes the bytes as before.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: no unit test; the mapping of the read results is a match, and
the backend only builds for OpenHarmony; see Validation.

Validation: `./mach check --ohos` with API 21 and with the dummy data source. A HiHope DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0, API 26), headless release builds (`./mach build --ohos --release --headless-binary`) of the series on the media cache and the media element that reads from it, against phase 1 (patches 91 to 96), each scenario in a fresh process, every run played to its end without a stall: a 90 s, 42.5 MB video takes 1 request and 42.5 MB with range support, at 1.5 MB/s and without range support, as before, with a peak RSS of 168 to 181 instead of 203 MB; a 30 s, 14.3 MB video takes 1 request and 14.3 MB for two passes of `loop` with or without range support instead of 3 requests and 42.8 MB, 1 request instead of 2 (27.5 MB) when seeking from 20 s back to 2 s, 1 instead of 2 (28.5 MB) for two elements, and 1 instead of 3 (42.8 MB) for three loads of the page; the 42.5 MB video takes 1 request for a seek back, for two passes of `loop`, for two elements and for two loads with or without range support. `./mach test-wpt --ohos --processes 1 --timeout-multiplier 2` of the series' 63 media tests: the same results as phase 1, with only the known unexpected OK of autoplay-default-permissions-policy, once the next patch reports a failure of AVPlayer once.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written and tested on a device by the agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
